WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:05.990
Hamilton fans have long loved the stage production,
but the film version on Disney Plus brings

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:08.930
whole new meaning to key moments in the story.

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:13.440
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing 11 surprising and

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.570
often satisfying ways the Hamilton movie enhances
or changes our experience of the musical.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:24.150
In other words, I'm explaining why it matters
who films, who edits, who directs your story.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:29.039
Just before we begin, many thanks to the sponsor
of this video, Full Sail University.

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:33.479
One of the nine cameras used to film the show
by cinematographer Declan Quinn was set up

00:00:33.479 --> 00:00:36.300
to capture overhead shots of the stage.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.589
Because that angle can be rather disorienting
for viewers, it's used sparingly during the

00:00:40.589 --> 00:00:41.760
final movie.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.730
But one of the moments where it does make
an appearance is during Satisfied.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:50.180
It's a fleeting shot from above but it heightens
our understanding of Angelica's distress

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:54.440
and also gives us a bird's-eye-view of the
clever lighting design where the lights converge

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:59.040
on Angelica as the words "drowning, drowning,
rewind" echo around the stage.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.479
All of that emphasises the sense of the world
closing in on Angelica and being at the centre

00:01:04.479 --> 00:01:07.600
of a figurative storm, an explosion of emotions.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.180
The film's quick overhead angle therefore
adds to the disorienting experience of the

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.460
whole song and its choreography as the lyrics
and dance moves rewind to earlier in the show.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.530
As Hamilton's film editor Jonah Moran told
The Art of the Cut podcast,

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:25.780
that view is something people never see in
the live stage show, even in the mezzanine

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.880
you still "don't really get that perspective".

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:32.180
There were lots of comments about Laurens
and Hamilton on my previous videos.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.170
Huge thanks to everyone for those.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:38.470
And many of you saw the often-lingering looks
exchanged between them as the show pointing

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.560
to their being more than just close friends.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.420
"Laurens, I like you a lot"
Indeed, in the film, the way the camera often

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:49.000
goes in for close-ups of the two of them seems
to highlight that, or at the very least leave

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.480
it open for interpretation.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.001
There are many examples of this in the first
act, including Stay Alive where the camera

00:01:55.001 --> 00:01:58.549
moves in when Laurens tells Hamilton he'll
fight Charles Lee,

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:03.940
then the camera goes in even closer, lingering
on Laurens' reaction to Hamilton's reply.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:08.099
The camera pushes in as well during the Story
of Tonight, which is also poignant for being

00:02:08.099 --> 00:02:11.170
one of several ways the show foreshadows both
Laurens' death

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:15.220
and the moment Hamilton discovers this in
Tomorrow There'll Be More of Us, which again

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.280
includes an extended moment between the two
men.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:23.120
In real life, Hamilton wrote many affectionate
letters to Laurens, including one where he

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.470
says,
"I wish, my dear Laurens, it might be in my

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:29.730
power, by action rather than words, to convince
you that I love you."

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:33.420
Historians tend to say they can't be definitive
whether the two were lovers.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:37.660
Ron Chernow, who wrote the biography which
inspired Lin-Manuel Miranda's musical, calls

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.970
their relationship
"the most intimate friendship of Hamilton's

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:43.450
life",
adding that "at the very least, we can say

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.560
that" Hamilton had "something like an adolescent
crush on his friend".

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.220
And according to Chernow, after Laurens died,
Hamilton "never again revealed his interior

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.730
life to another man as he had to Laurens",
"he shut off some compartment of his emotions

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:57.810
and never reopened it".

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:02.260
Lin-Manuel Miranda told EW that "when you
watch this movie, you have a house seat that

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.680
never existed",
and he's absolutely right as one of the cameras

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.800
had a view towards the audience thanks to
a hole cut into the back of the set!

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:14.820
And it's from that entirely fresh perspective
that we first meet King George, as he strides

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:18.810
past us, emerging from the shadows to be bathed
in red light.

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:23.800
That shot from behind really adds to the drama
of the King's entrance, nicely building up

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.490
to his first moment in the spotlight.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:31.000
During live performances, you might only spot
how dripping with madness King George is if

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:34.849
you're near enough to the stage;
but now that the film gives us all the best

00:03:34.849 --> 00:03:37.890
and same seat in the house, as the Hamilton
team put it,

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:43.780
no one can miss just how drenched in sarcasm
and saliva Jonathan Groff's performance gets.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:48.220
Yes, thanks to the filmmakers going right
in for lots of close-ups, everyone can now

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:52.670
appreciate Groffsauce, as Miranda nicknamed
him, as he enunciates and projects his way

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.019
around the stage!

00:03:54.019 --> 00:03:58.370
And Groff himself told Variety that he's always
been quite the spitter when it comes to performing

00:03:58.370 --> 00:03:59.370
live.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:03.860
"Hurricane" takes us inside Hamilton's head
and reveals his thinking behind making his

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.040
affair with Maria Reynolds public.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:11.260
As he reflects on his life, the show's lighting
and choreography put him in the eye of a storm,

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:15.840
still at first, then with people from his
past and present, including his enemies, friends,

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.099
lover, and mother spinning around him,
in a call-back to the literal hurricane that

00:04:20.099 --> 00:04:23.380
ultimately led him to leave his hometown for
America.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:27.389
The show's staging and story are underscored
by the film's camerawork.

00:04:27.389 --> 00:04:31.960
For director Thomas Kail, using a Steadicam
to film this scene meant they were able to

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:35.599
use the lens to
"create a closeness to Hamilton's decision"

00:04:35.599 --> 00:04:38.960
"by really being up close with Lin-Manuel
Miranda."

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:44.120
As Kail told EW, "There's a way that the Steadicam
floats which feels a little out of time.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:49.009
So, it felt like a chance to be with Hamilton
in the midst of this stillness before the

00:04:49.009 --> 00:04:50.009
explosion.

00:04:50.009 --> 00:04:53.729
And that creates a sense of anticipation for
what's about to come."

00:04:53.729 --> 00:04:58.169
Given the potentially disorienting effect
of overhead shots, it's totally fitting that

00:04:58.169 --> 00:05:02.900
they pop up again during The Reynolds Pamphlet
where we see the effects of Hamilton's document

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.389
about his extra-marital affair going public.

00:05:05.389 --> 00:05:10.300
The scene on stage is already a crazy one,
with several of the main cast and the ensemble

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.520
dancing around,
throwing leaflets into the air, and generally

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.800
revelling in Hamilton's self-inflicted downfall.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.719
For me, the overhead shot of Hamilton in the
middle of this chaos

00:05:19.719 --> 00:05:25.619
adds to the scene's sense of "deliriousness",
smug "celebration", "schadenfreude", and general

00:05:25.619 --> 00:05:29.800
"feeling that the world is crashing" down
around him, to use Miranda's words.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:34.830
The overhead shot highlights the fact that,
despite being surrounded by people, he's very

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:36.050
much alone.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:40.650
It does this by giving us a quick but clear
view of him as a lone figure in the centre

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:44.279
of the action,
being circled and taunted by a gleeful Jefferson

00:05:44.279 --> 00:05:48.409
and King George, while Madison tosses his
pamphlets up into the camera,

00:05:48.409 --> 00:05:51.529
and all the while they sing "Never gon' be
president now".

00:05:51.529 --> 00:05:56.680
On top of that, the aerial shot also reveals
one of the male dancers writhing around on

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:59.639
the floor,
seemingly mocking the kind of move that got

00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:02.479
Hamilton in this trouble in the first place.

00:06:02.479 --> 00:06:06.499
And all of that together dials up the wild
nature of the scene.

00:06:06.499 --> 00:06:11.069
Another way the film enhances the story is
using close-ups to reveal different perspectives

00:06:11.069 --> 00:06:13.699
by focusing on the actors' performances.

00:06:13.699 --> 00:06:18.539
During Helpless, just after Alexander and
Eliza first meet, we see Angelica's reaction

00:06:18.539 --> 00:06:20.809
from the side,
"I'll leave you to it",

00:06:20.809 --> 00:06:25.330
just for a second before the camera moves
to her sister, because this song is Eliza's

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:26.330
point of view.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:30.300
However, when we get to Satisfied, the story
gives us a different viewpoint on that evening,

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:35.849
and the film mirrors the way the narrative
refocuses by literally re-framing the situation

00:06:35.849 --> 00:06:39.729
so that now we clearly see the reaction on
Angelica's face in that moment,

00:06:39.729 --> 00:06:42.460
as this time the camera pushes in on her.

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:44.119
"I'll leave you to it."

00:06:44.119 --> 00:06:48.110
Overall, this whole number, and the way it's
shot and edited in the film, continues to

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:53.310
build the show's major theme that history
is different depending on who tells it.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:57.589
Of course, Satisfied is really emotional,
but much of it is performed at a break-neck

00:06:57.589 --> 00:07:00.289
pace,
to underline both Angelica's brilliance and

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:05.280
her distress, and the stage itself is frequently
full of activity during the song.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:09.949
To reflect that lyrical speed and action on
the stage, and "because Renée Elise Goldsberry's

00:07:09.949 --> 00:07:13.899
so expressive",
editor Jonah Moran chose to use lots of quick-cut

00:07:13.899 --> 00:07:17.740
close-ups so that
"you see that emotion and it flies right at

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:20.249
you, just like the song is flying at you."

00:07:20.249 --> 00:07:25.090
By the way, fun fact, director Thomas Kail
told Screenrant that the way Satisfied goes

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:29.629
"into the mind and the heart of Angelica Schuyler"
was heavily influenced by the scene in Pixar's

00:07:29.629 --> 00:07:32.349
Ratatouille where the "critic takes a bite
of the food.

00:07:32.349 --> 00:07:36.059
And in that moment, you go into his eye, and
you go back to see his childhood [...],

00:07:36.059 --> 00:07:40.000
and then he has this visceral and emotional
connection to the food."

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.849
When a Hamilfan asked Renée Elise Goldsberry
on twitter about her favourite hairstyle in

00:07:44.849 --> 00:07:49.330
the show, we got a little insight into another
thing that changed for the movie.

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:53.090
Angelica has several different looks in the
musical, and normally Goldsberry's favourite

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:57.959
is the high bun she wears towards the end,
however, for the filmed version, the actress

00:07:57.959 --> 00:08:00.240
chose to change up her early look.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:04.449
So, instead of wearing a wig as she does on
stage for the first part of the story, she

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:08.800
went with her own hair with some added volume
in the back, as she didn't want her usual

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:10.819
lace-front wig on screen.

00:08:10.819 --> 00:08:16.229
As I mentioned in my "Hamilton Things You
Missed" video, the lyrics run at a breath-takingly

00:08:16.229 --> 00:08:20.360
fast-and-furious speed,
so having access to captions right there on

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:24.490
the screen where it happens is great both
for those with hearing loss as well as everyone

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:25.490
else.

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:29.889
Even if you already know every line of the
show off by heart, seeing the words pop up

00:08:29.889 --> 00:08:33.700
on screen means,
for example, you can now easily see how specific

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:38.190
lyrics are tied to specific dance moves, gestures
and facial expressions.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:42.750
And that can be rather revealing because as
choreographer Andy Blankenbuehler has said,

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.890
every movement on stage "furthers the story
in some way".

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:49.730
And I talk more about that in my video on
the show's Secret Character.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:54.780
Also, in a production where the lyrics are
jam-packed with multiple meanings, historical

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:59.130
references, hat-tips to different musical
genres, witty retorts, and word-play,

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:04.460
being able to read each and every line on
screen makes it easier to notice more of those

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.220
amazing details.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:09.770
Of course, some but not all theatre productions
have surtitles,

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:14.000
but having captions right in front of you
during the film means you don't have to look

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.840
to the side or crick your neck to read what's
being said, as can be the case in the theatre.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:22.961
Speaking of lyrics, if you've seen the show
live or listened to the soundtrack, you'll

00:09:22.961 --> 00:09:26.780
likely notice that a couple of moments are
censored for language in the film on Disney

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:27.780
Plus.

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:32.350
That was to avoid getting an R rating, instead
of the PG-13 rating the film ended up with.

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:36.970
First of all, in the song "Yorktown", the
f-bomb is muted during Hercules Mulligan's

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:39.090
line
"When you knock me down, I get the **** back

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:40.090
up again!".

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:43.970
Then in the second act during "Washington
On Your Side", Jefferson and Madison's expletive

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:47.440
in the line
"Southern mother**** Democratic-Republicans"

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:51.970
is overlayed with sound, which Miranda called
a "record scratch", to erase it.

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:55.950
The change means the film is accessible to
a wider audience than it would have been otherwise,

00:09:55.950 --> 00:10:00.860
and, as Miranda told fans on twitter, "You
can sing whatever you like at home, even sync

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:01.860
up the album!"

